WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.732
NumPy includes many vectorized mathematical functions called universal functions, or ufuncs.

00:00:06.732 --> 00:00:11.172
These functions operate element by element across arrays.

00:00:11.172 --> 00:00:18.297
In the example, N P dot square root calculates the square root of every value in the array.

00:00:18.297 --> 00:00:22.312
N P dot log calculates the natural logarithm.

00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:28.007
We also apply N P dot absolute to an array containing negative values.

00:00:28.007 --> 00:00:30.867
Click Run and examine the outputs.

00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:34.377
The important idea is not to memorize every ufunc.

00:00:34.377 --> 00:00:37.152
Instead, recognize the pattern.

00:00:37.152 --> 00:00:42.742
You provide an array, and NumPy returns a new array containing the transformed values.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:50.527
Common functions include square root, log, exponential, absolute value, minimum, maximum, sine, cosine,

00:00:50.527 --> 00:00:51.397
and many others.

00:00:51.397 --> 00:00:59.682
In a real analytics workflow, these functions may be used when transforming skewed measurements,

00:00:59.682 --> 00:01:06.037
calculating distances, scaling variables, or preparing features for statistical models.

00:01:06.037 --> 00:01:12.971
When reading NumPy code, ask what transformation is being applied and whether it operates element by element.
